{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["I have already told you in the section of 'And these are the ordinances' that each and every commandment stands on its own; therefore there is, as it were, justification for joining the verses. Now the section of Kedoshim begins: 'A man, his mother and his father, you shall revere.' And it says [elsewhere] that the days of one who honors [them] will be lengthened; if so, the days of one who treats them lightly will be shortened. Therefore it says 'you shall revere.' The reason for mentioning the mother before the father is that the young child first recognizes only his mother, and afterward his father. And afterward, the Sabbath, for there is a commandment upon the young child to observe the Sabbath—and not so the other appointed times. And afterward he recognizes his Elohim, who rested on the seventh day: 'after him you shall not turn to the idols.' And reverence for the parents is known from tradition. It says 'a man' once, in abbreviated fashion. And it says 'you shall revere' in plural, for those who see are obligated to teach him and compel him—and likewise regarding Sabbath observance." "And the meaning of <b>I am YHWH your Elohim</b>: that you shall do as I did, for I rested from all labor."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:10:56.156569640Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}